WEBVTT

00:00:00.164 --> 00:00:03.392
Sequence analysis depends on order.

00:00:03.392 --> 00:00:06.072
A timestamp usually defines the main sequence.

00:00:06.072 --> 00:00:12.432
If two events share the same timestamp, a stable event ID can break the tie.

00:00:12.432 --> 00:00:16.952
Without a meaningful order, previous and next are undefined from an analytical perspective.

00:00:16.952 --> 00:00:20.332
Always treat the ordering clause as part of the business logic.

00:00:20.332 --> 00:00:28.242
LAG and LEAD compare a row with a previous or next row in a defined sequence.

00:00:28.242 --> 00:00:32.452
The sequence must be meaningful—usually time plus a stable tie-breaker.

00:00:32.452 --> 00:00:37.349
This is powerful for readmissions, repeat purchases, machine events, and state transitions.
